After trimming out the emotional parts there remains an important part.
useNet existed long before Google started displaying it in a web
interface.  UseNet has its own conventions and standards and they
are what they are for reasons.  Ignoring UseNet standards means you
have not bothered to learn what those reasons are.  So the issue
becomes - ignorance is curable with education, stupidity is not.  The
choise is with each and every poster.

Following UseNet standards is about far more than benefiting people
who use one sort of newsreader.  Simply put, groups where most
people follow the UseNet standards and where there is peer pressure
to follow them tend to have far higher quality discussions.  Read those
a few dozen newsgroups and comare.  RGRN has some of the best
signal to noise ratios in all of UseNet and also has some of the most
intense peer pressures to use UseNet standards.  These two are not
random.  They might be effect and cause they might not.  But they
absolutely share a common cause.

Quality of discussion flows from quality of material.  Quality of
material includes knowing what the standards are and conforming
to them most of the time.  Exactly the same as in music where
sonatas and symphonies have rules that are almost always
followed and occasionally broken.  Knowing when you're breaking
a convention makes all the difference.  Beethoven's Fifth broke a
convention.  Does anyone imagine he just randomly wrote music
having no idea what the convertions where?  It's all about knowing
what quality is and isn't.

Top posting isn't a sure-fire road to poor quality.  Think it through,
use mental discipline, practice your writing skills, and folks are
very unlikely to complain about your top-posting.  But do that and
what's the chance that you will ignore standards that are
conducive to organized response postings?  RGRN has quality
traffic because it's a mental game, and that leads to thinking through
issues, and that leads to conforming to UseNet standards, and
that leads to peer pressure to teach the new folks.



